---
import Presentation from "../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 6: Caliper Use &amp; Precision</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 6</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Caliper Use &amp; Precision</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Last Session's Question</h2>
              <p>Is precision measurement ever a safety issue?</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Mostly, today it's about quality &mdash; but a "close enough
                by eye" part that doesn't actually fit can absolutely become
                a safety issue later.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Why Precision Matters</h2>
              <p>
                "Close enough by eye" and "actually fits" are two different
                things.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Why Precision Matters</h2>
              <p>
                This is the foundation for two things coming up: Tolerance
                Concepts, and whether your parts actually mate together.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Meet the Caliper</h2>
              <p>
                A tool that measures far more precisely than a ruler &mdash;
                down to fractions of a millimeter.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Three Ways to Measure</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Outside</b> &mdash; length, width, outer diameter</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Inside</b> &mdash; the diameter of a hole</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Depth</b> &mdash; how deep a hole or slot goes</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Technique</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Zero it first</b> &mdash; every time, before you measure anything</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Close the jaws gently</b> &mdash; snug, not forced</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Read it straight-on</b> &mdash; not at an angle</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Activity</h2>
              <p>
                Everyone measures the <b class="text-sn-theme">same object</b>,
                multiple times. Then we compare results as a class.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Why Won't Everyone Get the Same Number?</h2>
              <p>Even measuring the exact same object.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Why Won't Everyone Get the Same Number?</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">How hard you closed the jaws</li>
                <li class="fragment">The angle you read the measurement from</li>
                <li class="fragment">Tiny inconsistencies in the object itself</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A little variation is normal.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Every measurement tool has limits. The goal isn't a perfect
                number &mdash; it's a consistent, trustworthy one.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A lot of variation means check your technique.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                If your own three trials don't agree with each other, that's
                the tool telling you something &mdash; not the object.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 7: Unit Conversion I</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Now that you can measure precisely, next we make sure those
                measurements get labeled and converted correctly.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Zero the Caliper</h3>
          <p>Before every measurement, close the jaws fully and confirm the reading is zero.</p>

          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [GIF PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Zeroing the Caliper]
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Measure Your Object</h3>
          <p>
            Using the object your instructor provides, take <b
              class="text-sn-theme">3 separate trials</b
            >. Close the jaws snug &mdash; not forced &mdash; and read the
            measurement straight-on.
          </p>

          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [GIF PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Proper Jaw Placement and Reading]
          </div>

          <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Trial</th>
                <th>Measurement</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>1</td>
                <td></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>2</td>
                <td></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>3</td>
                <td></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Compare as a Class</h3>
          <p>
            Add your measurements to the class data your instructor is
            collecting. Look at the full spread &mdash; smallest to largest
            &mdash; across everyone's trials.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Identify a Source of Variation</h3>
          <p>
            Name at least one likely reason the class's measurements aren't
            all identical, even though everyone measured the same object.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, check yourself against these:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Did you zero the caliper before your first trial?</li>
            <li>Did you take all 3 trials, not just one?</li>
            <li>
              Are your own 3 trials close to each other? If they're not, your
              technique &mdash; not the object &mdash; is the likely cause.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              What was the range &mdash; smallest to largest &mdash; across
              the whole class's measurements of the same object? Why do you
              think that range exists?
            </li>
            <li>
              Which of your own 3 trials do you trust most, and why?
            </li>
            <li>
              Tolerance Concepts (Session 9) asks how much variation is
              actually acceptable for a part to still work. Based on today,
              do you think all variation is a problem &mdash; or does it
              depend on what the part is for?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your trial data and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
